== Electoral system ==
The President of Slovenia is elected using the [[two-round system]]; if no candidate receives a majority of the vote in the first round, the top two candidates contest a runoff.<ref>{{cite web |date=7 August 1992 |title=Zakon o volitvah predsednika republike (ZVPR) |url=http://www.dvk.gov.si/PREDSEDNIK2007/ZVPR.html |accessdate=2008-09-05 |publisher=State Election Commission |language=sl}}</ref>

Under Slovenia's election law, candidates for president are required to meet one of three criteria:

* The support of ten members of the [[National Assembly of Slovenia|National Assembly]]
* The support of one or more political parties and either three members of the National Assembly or signatures from 3,000 voters
* Signatures from 5,000 voters

Each political party could support only one candidate.

== Candidates ==
*'''Nataša Pirc Musar''' is a former president of the [[Slovenian Red Cross]], a prominent human rights lawyer and was head of the data protection agency..<ref name=":2">{{Cite web |last=Stojanovic |first=Dusan |date=14 November 2022 |title=Slovenian president eager to work, gets Melania's congrats |url=https://abcnews.go.com/International/wireStory/slovenian-president-eager-work-melanias-congrats-93286609 |access-date=2022-11-15 |website=ABC News |language=en}}</ref> She stands for a center-left government and a pro-European Union policy.<ref name=":2" /> In view of [[Russo-Ukrainian War]], she believed that Russia broke [[international law]].<ref name=":2" /> Pirc Musar ran as an independent, but her campaign was supported by two former presidents, [[Milan Kučan]] and [[Danilo Türk]]<ref name=":3">{{Cite web |date=2022-11-14 |title=The left-liberal march forward: Pirc Musar is president of Slovenia |url=https://www.osw.waw.pl/en/publikacje/analyses/2022-11-14/left-liberal-march-forward-pirc-musar-president-slovenia |access-date=2022-11-15 |website=OSW Centre for Eastern Studies |language=en}}</ref> as well as the [[Pirate Party (Slovenia)|Pirate party]].<ref name=":3" />  

*'''Anže Logar''' was a longtime member of the [[Slovenian Democratic Party]] (SDS) and a former [[Ministry of Foreign Affairs (Slovenia)|Foreign Minister]]. He chose to officially be an independent candidate which was viewed as a distancing himself from the ousted government.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Maček |first=Sebastijan R. |date=2022-10-24 |title=Former foreign minister wins first round of presidential election |url=https://www.euractiv.com/section/all/short_news/former-foreign-minister-wins-first-round-of-presidential-election/ |access-date=2022-11-15 |website=Euractiv |language=en-GB}}</ref> 

*'''Milan Brglez''' was a candidate of the [[Social Democrats (Slovenia)|Social Democrats]] (SD) supported by the [[Freedom Movement (Slovenia)|Freedom Movement]],<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-09-22 |title=Freedom Movement to back Brglez in presidential race |url=https://sloveniatimes.com/freedom-movement-to-back-brglez-in-presidential-race/ |access-date=2022-11-15 |website=Slovenia Times |language=en-US}}</ref> and incumbent prime minister [[Robert Golob]].<ref name="LeMonde20221024"/>

