==Results==
In the first round [[Anže Logar]] received 34% of the voter share against 27% for [[Nataša Pirc Musar]].<ref name="LeMonde20221024">{{Cite news |date=2022-10-24 |title=Slovenia: Conservative Anze Logar leads first round of presidential election |language=en |work=Le Monde|url=https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2022/10/24/slovenia-conservative-anze-logar-leads-the-first-round-of-the-presidential-election-ahead-of-natasa-pirc-musar_6001508_4.html |access-date=2022-11-13}}</ref>  [[Milan Brglez]] was third with around 15%.<ref name="LeMonde20221024" /> For the second round Golob then turned his support to Pirc Musar.<ref name="LeMonde20221024" /> 

In the run-off, independent Pirc Musar was expected to win since August 2022.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Maček |first=Sebastijan R. |date=2022-08-23 |title=Former information commissioner top contender in Slovenia's presidential race |url=https://www.euractiv.com/section/politics/short_news/former-information-commissioner-top-contender-in-slovenias-presidential-race/ |access-date=2022-11-13 |website=www.euractiv.com |language=en-GB}}</ref> Logar also ran as an independent, but was a member of the [[Slovenian Democratic Party]] (SDS), which lost [[2022 Slovenian parliamentary election|parliamentary elections six months prior]].<ref name=":1">{{Cite news |date=2022-11-13 |title=Slovenia elects Natasa Pirc Musar to become first female president |language=en |work=Le Monde.fr |url=https://www.lemonde.fr/en/elections/article/2022/11/13/slovenia-elects-natasa-pirc-musar-to-become-first-female-president_6004144_84.html |access-date=2022-11-13}}</ref> Logar was therefore associated with Janša, who was accused with attempting to restrict the freedom of expression and undermine the independence of the judiciary during his term in office.<ref name=":1" />

In the evening of 13 November 2022, the electoral commission reported that Pirc Musar won with nearly 54% of the vote, after which Logar conceded defeat and congratulated Pirc Musar on her victory.<ref>{{Cite web |date=13 November 2022 |title=Rechtsanwältin Pirc-Musar wird Präsidentin Sloweniens |url=https://www.zeit.de/zustimmung?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.zeit.de%2Fnews%2F2022-11%2F13%2Fwahlkommission-slowenen-kueren-pirc-musar-zur-praesidentin |access-date=2022-11-13 |website=[[Die Zeit]]}}</ref> Pirc Musar will become Slovenia's first female president, commander-in-chief of the [[Slovenian Ground Force|Slovenian Army]] and also be responsible for appointing the head of the [[Bank of Slovenia|Central Bank of Slovenia]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Wright |first=George |date=2022-11-13 |title=Natasa Pirc Musar: Slovenia elects lawyer as first female president |language=en-GB |work=[[BBC News]] |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-63619160 |access-date=2022-11-15}}</ref>

